\title{The Subset Spectrum of a Finite Group Action:\\
Failure of Log-Concavity, a Rigidity Theorem,\\
and Sharp Guarded Substitutes}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let a finite group $G$ act on a finite set $X$ with $\abs{X}=n$. The
\emph{subset spectrum} of the action is the sequence $t_0,t_1,\dots,t_n$, where
$t_r$ is the number of $G$-orbits on the $r$-element subsets of $X$. We
investigate the natural conjecture that this sequence is always log-concave,
i.e.\ $t_r^2 \ge t_{r-1}t_{r+1}$ for $1 \le r < n$.

We prove that the conjecture is false, and we determine exactly when it holds.
The smallest counterexample is the regular action of the cyclic group of order
$4$ on four points, with spectrum $(1,1,2,1,1)$, which fails log-concavity at
$r=1$. More is true: a \emph{collapse principle} shows that two consecutive
spectrum values equal to $1$, together with log-concavity, force all subsequent
values to equal $1$. Since a transitive action has $t_0 = t_1 = 1$, this yields
a rigidity theorem: for a transitive action, the subset spectrum is log-concave
if and only if the action is set-transitive, i.e.\ $t_r = 1$ for every $r$.
Consequently a log-concave transitive action obeys $\binom{n}{r}\le\abs{G}$ for
every $r$, hence $\abs{G}\ge\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}$, and every regular
action on $n\ge 4$ points is a counterexample.

We supply two guarded substitutes valid for every finite action: the group-size
guard $t_{r-1}t_{r+1}\le\abs{G}^2t_r^2$, and the group-free shadow guard
$t_{r-1}t_{r+1}\le r(n-r)t_r^2$, the latter obtained from the extension and
deletion inequalities $t_{r+1}\le(n-r)t_r$ and $t_r\le(r+1)t_{r+1}$. Supporting
structural results---the complementation symmetry $t_r = t_{n-r}$, the
identification of $t_r$ with an orbit-quotient cardinality, Burnside's mass
formula for the spectrum, and a self-contained proof of log-concavity of the
binomial row---are developed along the way. We close with computational
evidence and three sharpened conjectures.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} subset spectrum, permutation group, orbit counting,
log-concavity, set-transitive group, homogeneous permutation group, shadow
inequality, Livingstone--Wagner theorem, binary necklaces.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

Log-concavity is one of the organising principles of modern enumerative
combinatorics. A sequence $a_0,\dots,a_n$ of nonnegative reals is
\emph{log-concave} if $a_r^2 \ge a_{r-1}a_{r+1}$ for $1 \le r < n$; equivalently
$\log a_r$ is concave where defined. Log-concave sequences without internal
zeros are unimodal, and log-concavity has been established for an extraordinary
variety of combinatorial sequences: the coefficients of the characteristic
polynomial of a matroid, matching polynomials, the numbers of independent sets
of each size in a matroid, and the coefficients of any polynomial with only real
nonpositive roots. Because so many natural counting sequences are log-concave,
it is reasonable to conjecture the property whenever a new family appears.

This paper concerns one such family. Given an action of a finite group $G$ on a
finite set $X$ of size $n$, the induced action on subsets partitions the
$r$-element subsets into orbits, and one may count them:
\[
  t_r := \#\{\, G\text{-orbits on the } r\text{-element subsets of } X \,\}.
\]
The resulting sequence $t_0,\dots,t_n$, the \emph{subset spectrum}, is a
classical invariant of a permutation group; it is the sequence appearing in the
Livingstone--Wagner monotonicity theorem, and for the cyclic group acting
regularly it enumerates binary necklaces by weight.

The subset spectrum is squeezed between two log-concave sequences: it is at most
the binomial row $\binom{n}{r}$ (which it equals when $G$ is trivial), at least
$\binom{n}{r}/\abs{G}$, symmetric under $r \mapsto n-r$, and constantly $1$ for
the full symmetric group. On this evidence one is led to the following.

\begin{conjecture}[Log-concavity of the spectrum]\label{conj:main}
For every action of a finite group on a finite set,
$t_r^2 \ge t_{r-1}t_{r+1}$ for all $1 \le r < n$.
\end{conjecture}

\subsection{Results}

Conjecture~\ref{conj:main} is false, and its failure is structural rather than
accidental. We establish:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item a complete structural theory of the spectrum (Section~\ref{sec:struct}):
    boundary values, support, complementation symmetry, the binomial sandwich,
    the orbit-quotient identity, Burnside's mass formula, and the
    characterisation of $t_r = 1$ as $r$-homogeneity;
  \item a refutation (Section~\ref{sec:refute}): the regular action of $C_4$ on
    four points has spectrum $(1,1,2,1,1)$;
  \item a collapse principle and a rigidity theorem
    (Section~\ref{sec:rigidity}): for transitive actions, log-concavity is
    \emph{equivalent} to set-transitivity, whence $\binom{n}{r}\le\abs{G}$ and
    the failure of log-concavity for all regular actions of degree $\ge 4$;
  \item two guarded substitutes (Section~\ref{sec:guards}):
    $t_{r-1}t_{r+1}\le\abs{G}^2 t_r^2$ and $t_{r-1}t_{r+1}\le r(n-r)t_r^2$;
  \item computational evidence and algorithms (Section~\ref{sec:comput}), and
    three sharpened conjectures (Section~\ref{sec:future}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Notation}

Throughout, $G$ is a finite group acting on a finite set $X$ with $n:=\abs{X}$.
For $g\in G$ and $s\subseteq X$ write $g\cdot s := \{g\cdot x : x\in s\}$. We
write $\binom{n}{r}$ for the binomial coefficient, with $\binom{n}{r}=0$ for
$r>n$. \emph{Transitive} means transitive on points; \emph{$r$-homogeneous}
means transitive on $r$-element subsets; \emph{set-transitive} means
$r$-homogeneous for every $0\le r\le n$; \emph{regular} means transitive with
$\abs{G}=\abs{X}$.

\section{The subset spectrum}

\begin{definition}[Induced action on subsets]
For $g\in G$ and $s\subseteq X$, put $g\cdot s = \{g\cdot x : x\in s\}$. Then
$1\cdot s = s$, $(gh)\cdot s = g\cdot(h\cdot s)$ and $\abs{g\cdot s}=\abs{s}$,
so this is an action of $G$ on the $r$-element subsets of $X$ for each $r$. It
commutes with complementation: $g\cdot(X\setminus s) = X\setminus(g\cdot s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Orbit of a subset]
For $s\subseteq X$ let $\Orb(s) := \{g\cdot s : g\in G\}$. Membership in an
orbit is an equivalence: if $u\in\Orb(s)$ then $\Orb(u)=\Orb(s)$ and
$\abs{u}=\abs{s}$. In particular $\abs{\Orb(s)}\le\abs{G}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Subset spectrum]
For $r\ge 0$ set
\[
  t_r \;=\; t_r(G,X) \;:=\; \#\bigl\{\,\Orb(s) : s\subseteq X,\ \abs{s}=r\,\bigr\},
\]
the number of distinct orbits of $r$-element subsets. The sequence
$(t_r)_{0\le r\le n}$ is the \emph{subset spectrum} of the action.
\end{definition}

The definition is directly computable, and it is intrinsic: it depends only on
the image of $G$ in the symmetric group on $X$.

\begin{proposition}[Spectrum as orbit quotient]\label{prop:quotient}
Let $\Sigma_r$ denote the set of $r$-element subsets of $X$ with its induced
$G$-action and $\Sigma_r/G$ the set of orbit classes. Then
$t_r=\abs{\Sigma_r/G}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The map $\Sigma_r/G \to \{\text{orbits}\}$, $[s]\mapsto\Orb(s)$, is well defined
(equivalent subsets have equal orbits) and injective (if $\Orb(s)=\Orb(u)$ then
$u=g\cdot s$ for some $g$, so $[s]=[u]$), and it is surjective by construction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Burnside mass formula for the spectrum]\label{thm:burnside}
\[
  t_r\cdot\abs{G} \;=\; \sum_{g\in G}\#\{\, s\subseteq X : \abs{s}=r,\ g\cdot s = s\,\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Proposition~\ref{prop:quotient}, $t_r$ is the number of $G$-orbits on
$\Sigma_r$. The orbit-counting lemma states that this number times $\abs{G}$
equals the number of incidences $(g,s)$ with $g\cdot s = s$; rewriting the
fixed-point set of $g$ in $\Sigma_r$ as the set of $r$-subsets fixed setwise by
$g$ gives the identity.
\end{proof}

\section{Structural theory}\label{sec:struct}

\subsection{Boundary values and support}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:boundary}
$t_0 = 1$ and $t_n = 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
There is exactly one $0$-element subset and exactly one $n$-element subset; each
forms a single orbit.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:support}
If $r>n$ then $t_r=0$; if $r\le n$ then $t_r\ge 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For $r>n$ there are no $r$-element subsets. For $r\le n$ there is at least one,
hence at least one orbit.
\end{proof}

Positivity on the support is essential below: it converts an upper bound of $1$
into an equality.

\subsection{Complementation symmetry}

\begin{theorem}[Complementation symmetry]\label{thm:compl}
For $0\le r\le n$ we have $t_r = t_{n-r}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Complementation $s\mapsto X\setminus s$ is a bijection from $r$-subsets to
$(n-r)$-subsets intertwining the $G$-actions, so it carries $\Orb(s)$ onto
$\Orb(X\setminus s)$; the induced map on orbits is a bijection because
complementation is an involution.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The binomial sandwich}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
For every $r$,
\[
  \binom{n}{r} \;\le\; \abs{G}\cdot t_r
  \qquad\text{and}\qquad
  t_r \;\le\; \binom{n}{r};
\]
equivalently $\binom{n}{r}/\abs{G} \le t_r \le \binom{n}{r}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper bound: $s\mapsto\Orb(s)$ maps the $\binom{n}{r}$ subsets of size $r$ onto
the set of orbits. Lower bound: the $r$-subsets are covered by the $t_r$ orbits,
each of size at most $\abs{G}$ since an orbit is the image of $G$ under
$g\mapsto g\cdot s$; hence $\binom{n}{r}\le t_r\abs{G}$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The two extreme actions}

\begin{proposition}[Trivial action]\label{prop:trivial}
If $g\cdot x = x$ for all $g\in G$, $x\in X$, then $t_r=\binom{n}{r}$ for all $r$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Every orbit is a singleton, so $s\mapsto\Orb(s)$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Symmetric group]\label{prop:sym}
If $G$ is the full symmetric group on $X$ then $t_r=1$ for every $r\le n$; that
is, the symmetric group is set-transitive.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Given $r$-subsets $s,u$, choose bijections $s\to u$ and $X\setminus s \to
X\setminus u$ (both exist since $\abs{s}=\abs{u}=r$); their union is a
permutation carrying $s$ to $u$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The value $1$ and homogeneity}

\begin{theorem}[$t_r=1$ is $r$-homogeneity]\label{thm:homog}
For $r\le n$, $t_r=1$ if and only if for all $s,u\subseteq X$ with
$\abs{s}=\abs{u}=r$ there is $g\in G$ with $g\cdot s = u$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If there is a single orbit then $\Orb(s)=\Orb(u)$, so $u=g\cdot s$ for some $g$.
Conversely, fix an $r$-subset $s_0$; every $r$-subset lies in $\Orb(s_0)$, so
there is a unique orbit.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[$t_1=1$ is transitivity]\label{cor:trans}
For nonempty $X$, $t_1=1$ if and only if the action is transitive on points.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:homog} with $r=1$ and identify $\{x\}$ with $x$, using
$g\cdot\{x\}=\{g\cdot x\}$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Log-concavity of the binomial row}

\begin{lemma}[Log-concavity of binomial coefficients]\label{lem:binom}
For all $n,k\ge 0$,
\[
  \binom{n}{k}\binom{n}{k+2} \;\le\; \binom{n}{k+1}^2 .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $k+1\ge n$ then $\binom{n}{k+2}=0$ and the claim is trivial; assume $k+1<n$.
Put $a=\binom{n}{k}$, $b=\binom{n}{k+1}$, $c=\binom{n}{k+2}$ and
$m=n-(k+1)\ge 1$. The identity $\binom{n}{j+1}(j+1)=\binom{n}{j}(n-j)$ gives
\[
  b(k+1) = a(n-k) = a(m+1), \qquad c(k+2) = b\,m .
\]
Hence
\[
  ac\,(k+1)(k+2) = \bigl(a(k+1)\bigr)\bigl(c(k+2)\bigr) = ab\,(k+1)m,
\]
\[
  b^2 (k+1)(k+2) = \bigl(b(k+1)\bigr)\bigl(b(k+2)\bigr) = ab\,(m+1)(k+2).
\]
Since $(m+1)(k+2)-(k+1)m = m+2k+4 > 0$, we get
$ac\,(k+1)(k+2)\le b^2(k+1)(k+2)$, and cancelling the positive factor
$(k+1)(k+2)$ gives $ac\le b^2$.
\end{proof}

\section{Refutation of the conjecture}\label{sec:refute}

\begin{definition}
The subset spectrum is \emph{log-concave} if $t_{r-1}t_{r+1}\le t_r^2$ for all
$1\le r<n$; equivalently, $t_kt_{k+2}\le t_{k+1}^2$ whenever $k+2\le n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[The property is not vacuous]\label{prop:nonvac}
For the trivial action the spectrum is log-concave, and likewise for the full
symmetric group.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:trivial} the trivial action has $t_r=\binom{n}{r}$, so
Lemma~\ref{lem:binom} applies; by Proposition~\ref{prop:sym} the symmetric group
has $t_r\equiv 1$, and $1\cdot 1\le 1^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Counterexample]\label{thm:counter}
Let $C_4$ act on $\mathbb{Z}/4$ by translation. Its subset spectrum is
\[
  (t_0,t_1,t_2,t_3,t_4) = (1,1,2,1,1),
\]
and log-concavity fails at $r=1$: $t_0t_2 = 2 > 1 = t_1^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$t_0=t_4=1$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:boundary}. The action is transitive, so
$t_1=1$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:trans}, and $t_3=t_1=1$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:compl}. The six $2$-subsets split into the four adjacent pairs
$\{i,i+1\}$ and the two opposite pairs $\{i,i+2\}$; translation preserves the
difference of the two elements up to sign, so these are distinct orbits and
$t_2=2$. Hence $t_0t_2 = 2 > 1 = t_1^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
It is not the case that the subset spectrum of every action of a finite group on
a finite set is log-concave.
\end{corollary}

The counterexample is minimal. For $n\le 3$ every spectrum is log-concave: the
possible spectra are $(1,1)$ and $(1,2,1)$ in degree $2$, and $(1,1,1,1)$,
$(1,2,2,1)$, $(1,3,3,1)$ in degree $3$. In degree $4$ an exhaustive scan of all
subgroups of the symmetric group shows that the failures are precisely the
transitive subgroups that are not $2$-homogeneous: the three cyclic groups $C_4$
and the three dihedral groups of a square (spectrum $(1,1,2,1,1)$) together with
the regular Klein four-group (spectrum $(1,1,3,1,1)$).

\section{Rigidity: when log-concavity does hold}\label{sec:rigidity}

\subsection{The collapse principle}

\begin{theorem}[Propagation of the value $1$]\label{thm:collapse}
Suppose $t_m = t_{m+1} = 1$ for some $m$, and suppose the spectrum is
log-concave. Then $t_r = 1$ for every $r$ with $m\le r\le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We show by induction on $j\ge 0$ that, as long as the indices stay $\le n$, the
pair $(t_{m+j},t_{m+j+1})$ equals $(1,1)$. The base case $j=0$ is the
hypothesis. For the inductive step, suppose $t_{m+j}=t_{m+j+1}=1$ and
$m+j+2\le n$. Log-concavity at index $m+j+1$ reads
$t_{m+j}\,t_{m+j+2}\le t_{m+j+1}^2 = 1$, so $t_{m+j+2}\le 1$; and
$t_{m+j+2}\ge 1$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:support}. Hence $t_{m+j+2}=1$ and the
pair $(t_{m+j+1},t_{m+j+2})$ is again $(1,1)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The rigidity theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity]\label{thm:rigid}
Suppose the action is transitive, i.e.\ $t_1=1$. Then the following are
equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item the subset spectrum is log-concave;
  \item $t_r = 1$ for every $0\le r\le n$, i.e.\ the action is set-transitive.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(2)$\Rightarrow$(1): if all $t_r=1$ then $t_{r-1}t_{r+1}=1=t_r^2$.
(1)$\Rightarrow$(2): if $n=0$ the claim is Proposition~\ref{prop:boundary};
otherwise $t_0=1$ and $t_1=1$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} with $m=0$ gives
$t_r=1$ for all $0\le r\le n$.
\end{proof}

Within the class of transitive actions, log-concavity therefore does not hold
generically with exceptions; it holds \emph{only} at the single most degenerate
point, where the spectrum is constant.

\subsection{Numerical consequences}

\begin{corollary}[Cardinality obstruction]\label{cor:card}
If the action is transitive and the spectrum is log-concave, then
$\binom{n}{r}\le\abs{G}$ for every $0\le r\le n$; in particular
$\abs{G}\ge\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}\sim 2^n\sqrt{2/(\pi n)}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid}, $t_r=1$; substitute into the lower bound
$\binom{n}{r}\le\abs{G}t_r$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Smallness forbids log-concavity]\label{cor:small}
If the action is transitive and $\abs{G}<\binom{n}{r}$ for some $r\le n$, then
the spectrum is not log-concave.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[All regular actions of degree $\ge 4$ fail]\label{thm:regular}
Let the action be regular, i.e.\ transitive with $\abs{G}=\abs{X}=n$. If
$n\ge 4$ then the spectrum is not log-concave.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $n\ge 4$ we have $\binom{n}{2}=n(n-1)/2>n$, so $\abs{G}=n<\binom{n}{2}$ and
Corollary~\ref{cor:small} applies with $r=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every $n\ge 4$ the regular action of the cyclic group $C_n$ on $n$ points
has a non-log-concave spectrum, as does every group of order $n\ge 4$ acting on
itself by translation.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Contrapositive form]\label{cor:contra}
A transitive action with $n\ge 2$ that is not $2$-homogeneous (i.e.\
$t_2\ge 2$) is not log-concave.
\end{corollary}

This is the practical criterion: transitive but not $2$-homogeneous is the
generic situation, so the conjecture fails generically. The groups that survive
are the set-transitive ones, a very restricted class consisting of the symmetric
and alternating groups in their natural actions together with a short list of
exceptional examples, the smallest of which is the affine group $\AGL(1,5)$ of
order $20$ acting on five points.

\section{Guarded substitutes valid for all actions}\label{sec:guards}

Since log-concavity with constant $1$ fails, we ask for the best constant $C$
with $t_{r-1}t_{r+1}\le C\,t_r^2$ for all finite actions.

\subsection{The group-size guard}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:groupguard}
For every finite action and every $r\ge 1$,
$t_{r-1}\,t_{r+1}\le\abs{G}^2\,t_r^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $r=k+1$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich},
$t_kt_{k+2}\le\binom{n}{k}\binom{n}{k+2}$, which is at most
$\binom{n}{k+1}^2$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:binom}; and
$\binom{n}{k+1}\le\abs{G}t_{k+1}$ again by Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}. Hence
$t_kt_{k+2}\le\abs{G}^2t_{k+1}^2$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Shadow inequalities}

\begin{theorem}[Extension bound]\label{thm:ext}
For every $r\ge 0$, $t_{r+1}\le(n-r)\,t_r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose for each orbit $\Orb$ of $r$-subsets a representative
$\rho(\Orb)\in\Orb$. We claim every orbit of $(r+1)$-subsets has the form
$\Orb\bigl(\rho(\Orb)\cup\{x\}\bigr)$ for some orbit $\Orb$ of $r$-subsets and
some $x\notin\rho(\Orb)$. Indeed, let $u$ be an $(r+1)$-subset, choose
$s\subseteq u$ with $\abs{s}=r$, let $\rho=\rho(\Orb(s))$, and pick $g$ with
$g\cdot s=\rho$. Then $\rho\subseteq g\cdot u$ and $\abs{g\cdot u}=r+1$, so
$g\cdot u = \rho\cup\{x\}$ for a unique $x\notin\rho$; and $g\cdot u$ lies in the
orbit of $u$. Thus the orbits of $(r+1)$-subsets are covered by the union over
the $t_r$ orbits $\Orb$ of the at most $n-r$ sets
$\Orb(\rho(\Orb)\cup\{x\})$, $x\notin\rho(\Orb)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Deletion bound]\label{thm:del}
For every $r$ with $r+1\le n$, $t_r\le(r+1)\,t_{r+1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:compl}, $t_r=t_{n-r}$ and $t_{r+1}=t_{n-r-1}$. The
extension bound at index $n-r-1$ reads
$t_{n-r}\le\bigl(n-(n-r-1)\bigr)t_{n-r-1}=(r+1)t_{n-r-1}$, which is the claim.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The group-free guard}

\begin{theorem}[Shadow guard]\label{thm:shadow}
For every finite action and all $1\le r<n$,
\[
  t_{r-1}\,t_{r+1} \;\le\; r\,(n-r)\,t_r^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $r=k+1$. Theorem~\ref{thm:del} at index $k$ gives $t_k\le(k+1)t_{k+1}$,
and Theorem~\ref{thm:ext} at index $k+1$ gives $t_{k+2}\le(n-k-1)t_{k+1}$.
Multiplying, $t_kt_{k+2}\le(k+1)(n-k-1)t_{k+1}^2 = r(n-r)t_r^2$.
\end{proof}

The bound is attained: for the regular action of the Klein four-group on four
points, with spectrum $(1,1,3,1,1)$, one has $t_0t_2 = 3 = 1\cdot 3\cdot 1^2$ at
$r=1$, with equality, so no smaller constant works at the boundary in this
generality. At $r=1$ the guard reads $t_2\le(n-1)t_1^2$; for the four-bead
necklace it gives $t_0t_2 = 2\le 3$ and for the ten-bead necklace
$t_0t_2 = 5\le 9$. In the interior of the range the bound is far looser, as the
data below show.

\section{Algorithms and computational evidence}\label{sec:comput}

\subsection{Computing the spectrum}

The definition is directly executable: enumerate the $\binom{n}{r}$ subsets of
size $r$, compute the orbit of each as $\{g\cdot s: g\in G\}$, and count distinct
orbits. This costs $O\!\left(\binom{n}{r}\abs{G}r\right)$ operations, and the
whole spectrum costs $O(2^n\abs{G}n)$. Replacing orbit computation by a
breadth-first search over subsets under a generating set $S$ reduces the factor
$\abs{G}$ to $\abs{S}$.

A third route uses Theorem~\ref{thm:burnside}. A permutation $g$ with $c_j(g)$
cycles of length $j$ fixes exactly $[x^r]\prod_{j\ge1}(1+x^j)^{c_j(g)}$ subsets
of size $r$, since a fixed subset is a union of cycles. Hence
\[
  t_r = \frac{1}{\abs{G}}\sum_{g\in G}[x^r]\prod_{j\ge 1}\bigl(1+x^j\bigr)^{c_j(g)},
\]
which is polynomial in $n$ once the cycle-type distribution of $G$ is known. For
$C_n$ this specialises to the classical necklace formula
\[
  t_r = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{d\mid\gcd(n,r)}\varphi(d)\binom{n/d}{r/d}.
\]

