\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Why the conjecture is plausible and why it fails}

Every heuristic for log-concavity here concerns the \emph{shape} of the
sequence: symmetric, unimodal, sandwiched between log-concave rows. None
suffices. Log-concavity constrains the \emph{second} differences of $\log t_r$,
and second differences are extremely sensitive to boundary normalisation. For a
transitive action the sequence begins $1,1$, so log-concavity at $r=1$ says
exactly $t_2\le 1$: not a regularity condition at all, but a homogeneity
condition, which the collapse principle then propagates across the whole range.

Contrast the Livingstone--Wagner theorem, which asserts $t_{r-1}\le t_r$
whenever $2r\le n$: a statement about \emph{first} differences. First
differences do not see the boundary normalisation as a constraint, and the
theorem holds for every finite permutation group. With Theorem~\ref{thm:compl}
it makes every subset spectrum unimodal with peak at $r=\lfloor n/2\rfloor$.
Since unimodality is normally derived from log-concavity, the present situation
inverts the usual hierarchy: the weaker conclusion is the true theorem, and the
stronger hypothesis is false.

\subsection{Sharpness of the guards}

The two guards have complementary weaknesses. The group guard is tight only in a
degenerate sense (for the trivial group it correctly returns constant $1$). The
shadow guard is nearly tight at the boundary and very loose in the interior,
because the extension and deletion bounds are never simultaneously tight: a
group that makes the extension map maximally non-injective must be large, and a
large group forces the deletion map to collapse.

\subsection{Interpretation}

The subset spectrum measures the resolving power of a symmetry group: how many
distinguishable configurations of each size survive the symmetry. Read this way,
Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} says that a symmetry group whose resolving power decays
log-concavely with configuration size must have no resolving power at all. In
applications---enumerating isomers under molecular symmetry, counting necklaces
and bracelets, classifying codes up to a symmetry group---the practically
relevant statements are the guarded ones; in particular Theorems~\ref{thm:ext}
and~\ref{thm:del} give cheap group-free a priori estimates for the number of
orbit types of size $r+1$ from the number of size $r$.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{conjecture}[Livingstone--Wagner monotonicity]
For every finite group action on $n$ points and every $r$ with $2r\le n$,
$t_{r-1}\le t_r$; consequently the spectrum is unimodal with peak at
$r=\lfloor n/2\rfloor$.
\end{conjecture}

Log-concavity is a statement about second differences of $\log t_r$, destroyed
by the boundary normalisation $t_0=t_1=1$; monotonicity is a statement about
first differences and is protected by injectivity of the inclusion map from
formal rational combinations of $r$-subsets to formal rational combinations of
$(r+1)$-subsets, whose Gram matrix is positive definite exactly for $2r\le n$.
All combinatorial infrastructure is available: the orbit-quotient identity, the
complementation symmetry, and the Burnside mass formula. The missing ingredient
is the standard fact that the dimension of the $G$-invariants of a permutation
module equals the number of orbits.

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp group-free guard]
For every finite action, $t_{r-1}t_{r+1}\le C\,t_r^2$ already holds with
$C=\max(r,n-r)$, and the exponent $1$ on $C$ cannot be lowered: there are
actions with $t_{r-1}t_{r+1}\ge c\,\max(r,n-r)\,t_r^2$ for an absolute constant
$c>0$.
\end{conjecture}

The two shadow inequalities are never simultaneously tight. The measured slack
is $2$ at $r=1$ for $C_6$ and $4$ for $C_{10}$: the bound $r(n-r)$ overshoots by
a factor $\approx n/2$ in the interior but is nearly tight at the boundary,
exactly the signature of a $\max(r,n-r)$ law.

\begin{conjecture}[Interior log-concavity for regular abelian actions]
For the regular action of a finite abelian group of order $n$, the truncated
spectrum $t_1,t_2,\dots,t_{n-1}$ is log-concave.
\end{conjecture}

Equivalently, the only failures for such actions are the two boundary indices
$r=1$ and $r=n-1$ forced by $t_0=t_1=1$. The data above confirm this for $C_n$,
$n\le 10$. If true, the original conjecture was not so much false as misindexed.

\section{Conclusion}

For a finite group $G$ acting on $n$ points, the subset spectrum $t_r$ is a
symmetric, positive, binomially sandwiched sequence which is \emph{not} in
general log-concave; the smallest counterexample is the four-bead necklace, with
spectrum $(1,1,2,1,1)$. Behind the failure lies an exact dichotomy: for
transitive actions, log-concavity is equivalent to set-transitivity and forces
$\abs{G}\ge\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}$, so every regular action of degree at
least $4$ is a counterexample. What survives universally are the two guards
$t_{r-1}t_{r+1}\le\abs{G}^2t_r^2$ and $t_{r-1}t_{r+1}\le r(n-r)t_r^2$, the
latter from a two-line shadow argument that is nearly tight precisely where the
conjecture fails. The correct general theorem in this circle of ideas is not
log-concavity but monotonicity up to the middle, and the sharp constant in the
guarded inequality is conjecturally $\max(r,n-r)$.

